<section>
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>Rules the pass must not break</h2>
    <p>The 2c design language is narrow. Every addition below reuses something already on the page rather than inventing a new visual idea.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="stack">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>Geometry</h3>
      <p>
        Canvas is 1180&times;880: a 66px header, a 1px divider, then
        <code>.tapt-desktop-main</code> at 1180&times;813. A design <span class="mono">y</span>
        maps to <span class="mono">y&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;66</span>. The right panel's usable height is
        <strong>787px</strong> and its usable width is <strong>466px</strong>; existing blocks
        size to 430–445px, so stay in that band.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>No <code>position: fixed</code>, ever</h3>
      <p>
        The canvas sits inside <code>transform: scale(…)</code>, so fixed positioning
        becomes scale-relative — the exact trap already recorded against
        <code>ReportModal</code>. Every overlay here is <code>position: absolute</code> inside a
        relative ancestor on the page. And no <code>window.confirm</code>: it paints browser
        chrome over the simulated 13&Prime; frame, so destructive confirmation is two-step
        and in-surface.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>Cascade</h3>
      <p>
        <code>.dt-cascade &gt; *</code> and <code>.dt-rise</code> ship <code>opacity: 0</code>
        under an <code>animation: … both</code> fill, so a block carrying either class that
        never animates is <strong>permanently invisible</strong>, not merely un-animated. New
        top-level blocks in <code>.pt-left</code> shift the <code>--dt-i</code> step numbering —
        the rail is 6, the panel is 7. Never transform a rail slot: it becomes the
        containing block for the absolutely-positioned rail and moves it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>Palette and prefix</h3>
      <p>
        Class prefix stays <code>pt-</code>, in the one page-scoped CSS template literal.
        New states reuse the file's ten colour constants: pressed is <code>ACTIVE</code>
        on <code>NAVY</code> text, unpressed is <code>ACCENT_SOFT</code> on transparent with a
        <span class="mono">rgba(94,158,255,0.5)</span> border, destructive is <code>RED</code>,
        confirmed is <code>GREEN</code>. New primary actions are ≥&nbsp;40px on their short
        axis for touch; new chips match the sizes already on the page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>The phases</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="phases">

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">1</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>Money correctness</h3><span class="chip chip-bug">bug</span><span class="label">no visual change</span></div>
        <p>
          The heroes and row amounts use <code>amountCents</code>, so a 4-way split with three
          shares paid reports its full value as outstanding. The phone uses the
          server-computed <code>owingCents</code>. The live set is a blacklist rather than the
          phone's whitelist, and row names resolve from the archived-filtered tenant list —
          so an archived tenant's live invoice renders as <span class="mono">"tenant"</span>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Extract the model to <code>desktop/data/property-terminal-model.ts</code>, matching the <code>desktop/data/*</code> convention, with a unit test beside it.</li>
          <li><code>owing(i) = i.owingCents ?? i.amountCents ?? 0</code> for both heroes <em>and</em> row amounts, so the column and the figures agree.</li>
          <li>Live set becomes the phone's whitelist: <span class="mono">pending_dispatch, dispatched, overdue, dispatch_failed</span>.</li>
          <li>Names come from the server's enriched <code>inv.tenantName</code>, with the tenant map as fallback.</li>
          <li>Keep bucketing <span class="mono">pending_dispatch</span> under <em>sent</em> — the phone's filter hides those rows, which is worse — but add a separate label so the row reads <strong>awaiting send</strong> instead of claiming it was sent.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">2</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>Send-flow safety</h3><span class="chip chip-bug">bug</span><span class="label">no new UI</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Keypad zero-guard.</strong> Confirming an empty keypad silently sets the amount to $0. Dim the confirm circle to <span class="mono">opacity&nbsp;0.45</span> and return early — mirroring <code>.pt-send-btn:disabled</code>, the file's existing disabled language.</li>
          <li><strong>Post-send reset.</strong> Tenant and amount both survive a successful send, so a second click issues a second invoice. Zero the amount on success; keep the tenant, which is the useful desktop state. The 1.6s flash covers the transition.</li>
          <li><strong>Description clobber.</strong> Picking a charge chip unconditionally overwrites typed text. Port the phone's rule: only overwrite when the field is empty or still holds a preset.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note">This phase creates <code>client/src/desktop/property-terminal.test.tsx</code>, modelled on <code>retail-terminal.test.tsx</code>. There is currently <strong>no test at all</strong> for this page — none of the nine defects behind this plan would have been caught by CI.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">3</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>Row actions</h3><span class="chip chip-new">new surface</span></div>
        <p>
          The phone has an action sheet on every request row. Desktop rows are inert
          <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>s, and <code>/invoices/:id/resend</code> and
          <code>/invoices/:id/void</code> have <strong>no caller anywhere in the desktop app</strong>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>An anchored popover in the left column</strong> — not a mode, not a modal. It reuses the panel already shipped as <code>.ph-scope-menu</code> on property home: <span class="mono">#0B1436</span>, radius 14, blue hairline, soft shadow.</li>
          <li><code>.pt-rows</code> scrolls, so a popover inside it clips. Render it as a child of <code>.pt-stack</code>, offset by <code>row.offsetTop − scrollTop</code>. Use <code>offsetTop</code>, not <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> — offset values are pre-scale and need no division by the canvas scale.</li>
          <li>Contents branch the way the phone's do: settled invoices get a message, charges get <em>resend link</em>, rent gets <em>edit amount &amp; resend</em>, then <em>mark received</em> and <em>cancel invoice</em>.</li>
          <li>Cancel is two-step in-surface: the popover swaps to a red "this can't be undone" with confirm / back.</li>
          <li>Both new mutations call <code>notifyIfBillingCardRequired</code> on failure. The phone omits it here, but the desktop send paths already do it and a silent 402 on a resend is the kind of thing this branch has been bitten by.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">4</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>Deep links and remind</h3><span class="chip chip-new">behaviour</span><span class="label">needs phase 3</span></div>
        <p>
          The desktop property home already links to <code>?mode=reminder</code> and
          <code>?mode=expense</code>, and the terminal reads no query parameters at all — so
          both quick actions silently open the default request panel.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Read them in a <code>useState</code> initializer, following <code>property-clients.tsx</code> and <code>trades-terminal.tsx</code>. Don't port the phone's 800ms <code>replaceState</code> strip — that hack exists because its route transition can discard the first mount.</li>
          <li><code>mode=expense</code> opens bill; <code>mode=reminder</code> filters to overdue and reveals the remind buttons; <code>stack=</code> sets the filter; <code>client=</code> preselects a tenant, matching 2b's param name.</li>
          <li>Remind button on overdue rows, on the phone's exact rule, calling Phase 3's resend mutation. ~28px tall so it sits inside the 40px row.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">5</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>Bill completeness</h3><span class="chip chip-new">controls</span></div>
        <p>
          Every desktop bill is hard-coded <em>due on receipt</em>, with no way to say otherwise,
          and can't carry the supporting document the phone uploads. The attach omission was
          logged as deliberate — "worth its own pass rather than a cramped port". This is that pass.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Due chips</strong> in the existing label-then-chips rhythm: <em>on receipt / in 7 days / in 14 days</em>, defaulting to 7, in a grid mirroring <code>.pt-freq-chips</code> so the two screens' chip rows are the same object.</li>
          <li><strong>Attach invoice</strong> — a label-wrapped hidden file input styled as a 50px dashed row at radius 12, matching <code>.pt-field-row</code> rather than the phone's 16. Uploads on pick with the phone's 20MB guard; attached state swaps to filename plus <em>remove</em>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note"><strong>Vertical budget.</strong> Bill mode currently uses ≈468px of the 787px panel. Due adds ≈96, attach ≈102 → ≈666, leaving 121. Phase 6's split row takes ≈62 of that → ≈728, 59px spare. Reclaim 40px while you're in there by tightening <code>.pt-bill</code>'s top margin and the four label margins. <strong>Screenshot both device classes and confirm nothing crosses 813px</strong> — 3c's compose block failed exactly here.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">6</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>Split</h3><span class="chip chip-new">controls</span><span class="chip chip-blocked">question 1</span></div>
        <p>
          Desktop hard-codes <code>splitEnabled: false</code> on both mutations and renders no
          split state at all. The phone offers split on a rent request and on a bill, and
          shows <span class="mono">paid/count split</span> plus "left of&nbsp;$X" on part-paid rows.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>One more <code>.pt-field-row</code> on both the request and bill panels: split glyph, "split this bill", toggle. It reuses the 54px row already used twice above it, so the lower block reads as three consistent rows plus chips.</li>
          <li><code>.pt-switch</code> is the one genuinely new component in this plan — worth defining rather than substituting a chip, because Phase 7 reuses it.</li>
          <li>In the request list: a green <span class="mono">paid/count split</span> badge, and a "left of&nbsp;$X" caption stacked under the amount the way <code>.pt-tc-right</code> already stacks amount over caption.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">7</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>Automation mode</h3><span class="chip chip-new">new mode</span></div>
        <p>
          <strong>Desktop can create recurring rent and then gives the merchant no way to see,
          pause or cancel it.</strong> The desktop <em>trades</em> terminal already ships this
          surface, so it's a desktop-internal inconsistency as much as a phone gap.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A sixth rail button</strong>, and tighten the rail's gap from 40px to 32px at the same time: six buttons at gap 40 makes the rail 547px tall ending at y=748 and visibly bottom-heavy; at 32 it's 507px ending at y=708, growing by one button height rather than a button plus a gap.</li>
          <li><code>usePropertySchedules()</code> already exists in <code>property-data.ts</code> — use it, don't add a query. One new query for reminder settings, optimistic like the phone's.</li>
          <li><strong>Overdue reminders</strong> block: title, sub, the Phase 6 toggle, then <em>remind after</em> / <em>repeat every</em> / <em>max reminders</em> chip rows and the summary sentence. Render it in the page's blue language, <strong>not</strong> the phone's amber card — amber on this page means <em>overdue</em>, and an amber panel here would read as an alert.</li>
          <li><strong>Active schedules</strong> list reusing <code>.pt-paid-row</code>'s geometry, with a state pill, next-run date, pause/resume and a two-step cancel.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">8</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>External reference</h3><span class="chip chip-new">control</span></div>
        <p>
          <code>markPaid</code> hard-codes the reference to <code>null</code> while the column
          exists and the phone captures it. Keep the flat list — clicking a row's check
          <strong>expands that row in place</strong> with a slim input and confirm / cancel,
          other rows untouched, one row open at a time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-n">9</div>
      <div class="phase-body">
        <div class="phase-top"><h3>Scope chip</h3><span class="chip chip-clean">cleanup</span><span class="chip chip-blocked">question 3</span></div>
        <p>
          The "all properties" chip has no <code>onClick</code> and no state — a dead control,
          while property home has the identical chip fully wired.
          <strong>Recommendation: wire it.</strong> Port the home's scope pattern, derive the
          address list from <em>active</em> tenants only so the known
          "archived-only properties remain selectable" defect isn't reintroduced, and let both
          heroes, the request list and the mark-paid list follow the scope. The tenant picker
          does not — you may need to bill a tenant outside the current scope.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

  </div>
</section>

